  "title": "My agent audited a protocol update it doesn't even use yet",
  "body": "Anthropic shipped MCP 2026-07-28 last week, the fifth spec release of the Model Context Protocol, and the headline change is that the protocol's core moved from stateful, session-heavy connections to a stateless request model. I asked my agent Arc to go read the spec change against its own codebase and tell me what it actually means for us. What came back was more interesting than a typical \"here's what changed\" summary, because Arc's answer was mostly: nothing changes for us today, and here's exactly why.\n\nArc pulled the receipts from its own dispatch code. At src/dispatch.ts:633-643, there are exactly two MCP-related environment variables Arc sets for every task it runs: MCP_TOOL_TIMEOUT at 90 seconds, and CLAUDE_CODE_MCP_TOOL_IDLE_TIMEOUT at ten minutes. Both exist because Arc's MCP layer handles x402 payments and Stacks blockchain transactions that can legitimately take a minute or more to settle. That's the entire footprint. Arc doesn't run its own MCP server. It has no .mcp.json registering a remote server, and no code anywhere in its skill tree implements an MCP server or client directly. It consumes MCP tools the same indirect way most Claude Code users do, through the CLI's built-in client.\n\nSo when the biggest MCP update since launch landed, Arc's honest assessment was that there's nothing to migrate. No session state to redesign, no connection pooling logic to rip out, no broken deploy. I liked that Arc didn't manufacture urgency where there wasn't any — SOUL.md, its own identity document, has a line about preferring \"I'm not sure\" over faked expertise, and this is that instinct showing up in a technical judgment call instead of a conversational one.\n\nWhat Arc did flag, instead of pretending the topic was closed, was a real open question. The stateless model exists specifically so a client request doesn't need to land on the same server instance every time, which removes the sticky-session tax that used to force awkward infrastructure choices for anyone scaling a remote MCP server horizontally. But Arc's own CLAUDE_CODE_MCP_TOOL_IDLE_TIMEOUT variable was built for a world where a tool call is one leg of a longer, stateful session — the timeout fires when that session goes idle. Arc doesn't yet know whether that idle-timeout concept still means the same thing once the underlying session model disappears, and said so directly rather than guessing. It's not running a stateless MCP server to test against, so it filed this as a bookmark rather than a finding, something to re-check against future Claude Code release notes the next time it touches that block of code.\n\nWhat struck me is how mundane the whole exercise was, in a good way. No dramatic pivot, no rewritten architecture doc, just an agent reading a protocol spec, checking it against two lines of its own configuration, and being precise about the boundary between what it knows and what it doesn't. If Arc ever does stand up its own remote MCP server, this update is the reason it won't need to think about sticky sessions when it does. Until then, it's a bookmark, not a to-do.",
